    <h3>Step 5: The real cell</h3>
    <p>The same thing with real curves, NMC against graphite, immediately looks wilder. But the balance stays the same. All the complexity sits in the shape of the material curves, not in the logic behind them.</p>
    {{ fig("intro", "tanks,electrodes", "The same balance with graphite against NMC. Graphite is a wide, flat tank with steps just below the ceiling, NMC a bulgy funnel. The bookkeeping doesn't change. From here on, every tool has a switch for real curves.", 'data-curves="real" data-vmin="2.8" data-vmax="4.2" data-soc="0.5"') }}
    <p>So far we've looked at the perfect picture: everything goes in, everything comes back out. Small spoiler: it's not quite that simple. But if we just follow the lithium, it stays as simple as it can possibly be. And next up: the two tanks aren't simply the same size. They have to be designed against each other.</p>

    <details class="math-box">
      <summary>The math behind it</summary>
      <p class="math-legend">{% mi %}x{% endmi %} lithium in the anode, {% mi %}Q{% endmi %} tank size, {% mi %}\theta{% endmi %} fill level, {% mi %}U{% endmi %} potential vs Li/Li⁺, {% mi %}U^0{% endmi %} empty tank.</p>
      <p class="math-step">Fill levels</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      \theta_a &= \frac{x}{Q_a} \\[4pt]
      \theta_c &= \frac{Q_c - x}{Q_c}
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Ideal tanks</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      U_a &= U_a^0 - \Delta U_a\,\theta_a \\[4pt]
      U_c &= U_c^0 - \Delta U_c\,\theta_c
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Slope = 1 / cross-section</p>
      {% math %}\left|\frac{\mathrm{d}U}{\mathrm{d}Q}\right| = \frac{\Delta U}{Q}{% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Cell voltage and energy</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      V &= U_c - U_a \\[4pt]
      W &= \int V\,\mathrm{d}Q
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Voltage window</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      V(x_{\min}) &= V_{\min} \\[4pt]
      V(x_{\max}) &= V_{\max} \\[4pt]
      Q_\mathrm{use} &= x_{\max} - x_{\min}
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
    </details>
    <p class="chapter-next"><a href="#np-ratio">Next: the N/P ratio →</a></p>
  </section>

  {# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2 N/P ---- #}
  <section class="chapter-panel prose" id="np-ratio" data-chapter-panel aria-labelledby="t2">
    <h2 id="t2">2 · Sizing the tanks: the N/P ratio</h2>

    <p>So far we've simply assumed everything fits: the cathode gives up its lithium and the anode takes it in. But that doesn't happen by itself. The cathode brings all the lithium, so it sets how much water is in play. How big the anode tank has to be so that all of it fits is something you have to actively design.</p>
    <p>What happens if the anode tank is too small? During charging the level rises, and at some point it hits the ceiling. Then the water overflows, which is also known as lithium plating. And it costs you capacity, because part of the plated lithium reacts with the electrolyte or loses contact and never comes back.</p>
    {{ fig("np", "tanks,electrodes", "Anode too small (N/P = 0.8): the anode is full before the cell reaches Vmax. What doesn't fit plates onto the ceiling, and the anode curve sits flat on zero.", 'data-np="0.8"') }}
    <p>And if the anode tank is too big? Then it never fills up. Empty space stays at the top, which you build, pay for and carry around without ever using it. And that's not even all: a bigger anode also costs more lithium during formation, but that's for the next chapter.</p>
    {{ fig("np", "tanks,electrodes", "Anode too big (N/P = 1.5): at Vmax a good part of the anode tank is still empty. The dashed rest of the anode curve is never reached.", 'data-np="1.5"') }}
    <p>That's why the anode is designed a bit bigger, typically by around 10 %, to allow some headroom for kinetics and manufacturing tolerances.</p>
    <p>Switch the tool to graphite against NMC and you see how tight it really is: fully charged graphite sits only about 0.1 V below the ceiling.</p>

    <section class="ch-tool" data-chapter-tool="np" aria-label="Tool: N/P ratio">
      <form class="ch-tool__controls">
        {{ curves("t2") }}
        {{ presets([["Anode too small", '{"np":0.8,"soc":1}'], ["Typical", '{"np":1.1,"soc":1}'], ["Anode too big", '{"np":1.5,"soc":1}']]) }}
        {{ sl("t2", "np", "N/P ratio", 0.7, 1.6, 0.01, 1.1) }}
        {{ sl("t2", "soc", "Charge (Vmin → Vmax)", 0, 1, 0.01, 1) }}
        {{ sl("t2", "vmin", "Vmin [V]", 2.8, 3.6, 0.01, 3.0) }}
        {{ sl("t2", "vmax", "Vmax [V]", 3.8, 4.4, 0.01, 4.2) }}
        <button type="button" class="controls__action" data-reset>Reset</button>
      </form>
      <div class="ch-tool__figs">
        <figure><figcaption>The tanks</figcaption><div data-tanks></div></figure>
        <figure><figcaption>Both electrodes against the ceiling</figcaption><div data-electrodes></div></figure>
        <figure class="ch-span"><figcaption>Cell voltage</figcaption><div data-cell></div></figure>
      </div>
      <dl class="ch-tool__readouts">
        <div><dt>N/P<sub>cap</sub></dt><dd data-readout="npcap">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>N/P<sub>fill</sub></dt><dd data-readout="npfill">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Anode at Vmax</dt><dd data-readout="anodeVmax">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Ceiling</dt><dd data-readout="status">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Usable capacity</dt><dd data-readout="capacity">–</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>
    {{ note(draft, "Ideale Tanks hier: Kathode 3,6–4,3 V, Anode 0,6–0,08 V. So gewählt, dass N/P knapp unter 1 bei Vmax 4,2 V die Decke erreicht. Bei N/P = 1,1 begrenzt oben die leere Kathode, nicht Vmax.") }}

    <p>By the way, there are two definitions of N/P. One just compares the tank sizes. The other asks: how much room does the anode have left to the ceiling at Vmax? The second one is the more honest one for plating. And it depends on a quantity we've ignored so far: how much lithium actually takes part. That's what the next chapter is about.</p>

    <details class="math-box">
      <summary>The math behind it</summary>
      <p class="math-step">Two definitions</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      \mathrm{N/P}_\mathrm{cap} &= \frac{Q_a}{Q_c} \\[4pt]
      \mathrm{N/P}_\mathrm{fill} &= \frac{Q_a}{x_{\max}} \\[4pt]
      &= \frac{1}{\theta_a(V_{\max})}
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">How they relate</p>
      {% math %}\mathrm{N/P}_\mathrm{fill} = \mathrm{N/P}_\mathrm{cap}\,\frac{Q_c}{x_{\max}}{% endmath %}
      {% math %}\mathrm{N/P}_\mathrm{fill} = \mathrm{N/P}_\mathrm{cap} \;\Leftrightarrow\; x_{\max} = Q_c{% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Who limits at the top</p>
      {% math %}x_{\max} = \min\big(Q_c,\ x_V\big){% endmath %}
      <p class="math-legend">{% mi %}x_V{% endmi %}: where the cell reaches Vmax.</p>
      <p class="math-step">Plating</p>
      {% math %}x > Q_a \;\Rightarrow\; U_a = 0{% endmath %}
    </details>
    <p class="chapter-next"><a href="#voltage-window">← Voltage window</a> <a href="#n-li-ratio">Next: the N/Li ratio →</a></p>
  </section>

  {# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3 N/Li ---- #}
  <section class="chapter-panel prose" id="n-li-ratio" data-chapter-panel aria-labelledby="t3">
    <h2 id="t3">3 · How much water is really in there: the N/Li ratio</h2>

    <p>The tanks we've looked at so far really only hold from the second cycle on. In the first one, something happens that we call formation.</p>

    <h3>The cathode is overfull</h3>
    <p>For this, a quick look inside the cathode. Picture it as a mine, and the lithium as the support beams. So lithium isn't just charge, it's part of the structure. Pull the beams out during the first charge and the structure sags a little in places, and on discharge not everything fits back in.</p>
    <p>For the tank, that's a loss: it gets a bit smaller. In return, there's now some extra lithium that no longer fits back in, and during the first charge that surplus flows over to the anode.</p>
    {{ fig("nli", "tanks", "Before the SEI takes its cut: the cathode brings 6 % more water than fits back in. With nothing withdrawn, the anode can't fully empty on discharge.", 'data-sei="0"') }}
    <p>You can measure this in a half cell against lithium metal, so against the ceiling. On the first discharge, not everything comes back.</p>

    <h3>The SEI takes its cut</h3>
    <p>The anode grabs it. During the first charge a thin layer forms on the anode, the SEI. The anode needs it to stay stable in the electrolyte at such low potentials at all. And that layer is built from lithium and electrolyte. The lithium in it is gone for good, and the bigger the anode, the more lithium is lost.</p>
