    <h3>The lithium account</h3>
    <p>Let's do the books.</p>
    {{ fig("nli", "account", "The lithium account from the cathode in operation to the lithium in operation. The dashed line is the room in the cathode.") }}
    <p>On the account sits what the cathode can take back, plus the bonus it brings from the factory by being overfull. The SEI makes a one-off withdrawal, and that's the real loss. The bonus covers part of it, but usually not all. So in the end you're usually short of lithium, not of room.</p>
    <p>N/P tells you how big the tanks are. N/Li tells you how much water is really in there.{{ draftTag(draft) }}</p>

    <h3>What this does to the plot</h3>
    <p>In the plot, the two lines shift against each other. On discharge the anode runs empty before the cathode is full again, and a gap stays in the cathode. On charge the anode doesn't get as close to the ceiling anymore. Lithium has always been the limit. What's new is that there's now less of it than fits into the cathode.</p>
    {{ fig("nli", "tanks", "Discharged after formation: the anode is empty, and a gap stays in the cathode.") }}
    <p>So N/Li is a design lever of its own, and one that protects better against plating. You see it most clearly in a comparison: NMC against graphite and LFP against graphite at the same N/P, and the same again with prelithiation.</p>
    <figure class="ch-fig ch-compare" data-chapter-compare>
      <div class="ch-fig__parts" data-figure-parts></div>
      <figcaption>Both cells charged to Vmax, same N/P of 1.1 and the same SEI. NMC brings a big formation bonus that roughly covers the SEI, LFP brings very little, so its anode stays emptier.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    {{ note(draft, "Vergleichsbild NMC/LFP von Claude ergänzt (im Diktat nur Text). Annahmen: SEI 8 % der Anode, Bonus NMC 9 %, LFP 2 %, Vmax 4,2 V bzw. 3,65 V. Dasselbe Bild geht in den LinkedIn-Post.") }}

    <section class="ch-tool" data-chapter-tool="nli" aria-label="Tool: lithium account and N/Li ratio">
      <form class="ch-tool__controls">
        {{ curves("t3") }}
        {{ presets([["Typical", '{"np":1.2,"bonus":6,"sei":10,"prelith":0}'], ["No SEI", '{"sei":0,"prelith":0}'], ["Big anode", '{"np":1.5,"prelith":0}'], ["Prelithiated", '{"np":1.1,"bonus":6,"sei":10,"prelith":1.1,"soc":1}'], ["Too much", '{"np":1.1,"bonus":6,"sei":10,"prelith":2,"soc":1}']]) }}
        {{ sl("t3", "np", "N/P ratio", 1.0, 1.6, 0.01, 1.2) }}
        {{ sl("t3", "bonus", "Formation bonus of the cathode [%]", 0, 15, 0.5, 6, 1) }}
        {{ sl("t3", "sei", "SEI loss [% of anode capacity]", 0, 20, 0.5, 10, 1) }}
        {{ sl("t3", "prelith", "Prelithiation [Ah]", 0, 2.5, 0.05, 0) }}
        {{ sl("t3", "soc", "Charge (Vmin → Vmax)", 0, 1, 0.01, 0) }}
        {{ sl("t3", "vmin", "Vmin [V]", 2.8, 3.6, 0.01, 3.0) }}
        {{ sl("t3", "vmax", "Vmax [V]", 3.8, 4.4, 0.01, 4.2) }}
        <button type="button" class="controls__action" data-reset>Reset</button>
      </form>
      <div class="ch-tool__figs">
        <figure><figcaption>The tanks</figcaption><div data-tanks></div></figure>
        <figure><figcaption>Both electrodes against the ceiling</figcaption><div data-electrodes></div></figure>
        <figure><figcaption>The lithium account (cathode 10 Ah)</figcaption><div data-account></div></figure>
        <figure><figcaption>Cell voltage</figcaption><div data-cell></div></figure>
      </div>
      <dl class="ch-tool__readouts">
        <div><dt>Lithium in operation</dt><dd data-readout="li">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>N/Li</dt><dd data-readout="nli">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Gap in the cathode at Vmin</dt><dd data-readout="gap">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Anode at Vmax</dt><dd data-readout="anodeVmax">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Scarcest item</dt><dd data-readout="limit">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Usable capacity</dt><dd data-readout="capacity">–</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <h3>Outlook: prelithiation</h3>
    <p>Prelithiation is popular when the SEI takes too much or the books don't balance otherwise. You put extra lithium into the anode. If you then design naively by N/P alone, you slide into plating very quickly. This is where N/Li really matters.</p>
    <p>A quick example: cathode 10 Ah, anode 11 Ah, so N/P 1.1. Without prelithiation, the bonus brings 0.6 Ah and the SEI takes 1.1 Ah. That leaves 9.5 Ah of lithium, N/Li ≈ 1.16. Now we offset the SEI completely with 1.1 Ah of prelithiation: 10.6 Ah of lithium, N/Li ≈ 1.04. N/P still says 1.1 and looks relaxed, but the margin to the ceiling has shrunk from 16 % to 4 %. Add a little more and N/Li drops below 1, and the cell plates.</p>

    <p>And if the SEI just keeps withdrawing after formation, cycle after cycle? Then N/Li keeps shifting. Welcome to aging.</p>

    <details class="math-box">
      <summary>The math behind it</summary>
      <p class="math-legend">{% mi %}B{% endmi %} formation bonus, {% mi %}P{% endmi %} prelithiation, {% mi %}Q_\mathrm{SEI}{% endmi %} SEI loss, {% mi %}\mathrm{Li}{% endmi %} lithium in operation.</p>
      <p class="math-step">The lithium account</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      Q_\mathrm{start} &= Q_{c,\mathrm{rev}} + B \\[4pt]
      \mathrm{Li} &= Q_{c,\mathrm{rev}} + B + P - Q_\mathrm{SEI} \\[4pt]
      \mathrm{N/Li} &= \frac{Q_a}{\mathrm{Li}}
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Shift of the lines (gap in the cathode at Vmin)</p>
      {% math %}\Delta x = Q_{c,\mathrm{rev}} - \mathrm{Li}{% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Example</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      \mathrm{Li} &= 10 + 0.6 - 1.1 = 9.5\ \mathrm{Ah} \\[4pt]
      \mathrm{N/Li} &= 11 / 9.5 \approx 1.16 \\[4pt]
      \mathrm{Li}_P &= 9.5 + 1.1 = 10.6\ \mathrm{Ah} \\[4pt]
      \mathrm{N/Li}_P &= 11 / 10.6 \approx 1.04
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
    </details>
    <p class="chapter-next"><a href="#np-ratio">← N/P ratio</a> <a href="#aging">Next: aging →</a></p>
  </section>

  {# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4 Alterung ---- #}
  <section class="chapter-panel prose" id="aging" data-chapter-panel aria-labelledby="t4">
    <h2 id="t4">4 · Three accounts: aging modes</h2>

    <p>Aging is genuinely complicated, there are hundreds of different mechanisms. In equilibrium, though, it gets simple again. A cell is just lithium, two tanks and bookkeeping, so it can only lose three things: water, or room in one of the two tanks, meaning active material.</p>
    <p>From the outside, though, you can only measure one thing: capacity. How do the three modes show up in it? An example: a cell with N/P = 1.1 and N/Li = 1.2 loses 5 % of its anode, 3 % of its cathode and 2 % of its lithium. How much capacity does it lose?</p>
    <div class="quiz" data-quiz="aging-loss">
      <p class="quiz__question">How much capacity does the cell lose?</p>
      <div class="quiz__options">
        <button type="button" data-answer="10" data-correct="false" data-no="That's adding them up. Read on.">10 %</button>
        <button type="button" data-answer="5" data-correct="false" data-no="Not quite. Read on.">5 %</button>
        <button type="button" data-answer="2" data-correct="true" data-yes="Right. Only the scarcest item counts.">2 %</button>
      </div>
      <p class="quiz__feedback" data-quiz-feedback role="status" hidden></p>
    </div>
    <p>The answer is 2 %. Capacity is what the scarcest of the three items still lets through between Vmin and Vmax, and here that's the lithium. Losses don't add up.</p>

    <p>Both tanks have a buffer. At the anode it's designed in, that's the excess from the N/P ratio. At the cathode it comes from formation, as the gap the missing lithium leaves behind. Most of the time lithium is the scarcest item. As long as that's true, losses at the anode or cathode only eat into buffer at first, and from the outside you see nothing.</p>

    <section class="ch-tool" data-chapter-tool="aging" aria-label="Tool: aging modes">
      <form class="ch-tool__controls">
        {{ curves("t4") }}
        {{ presets([["Anode loses", '{"lama":25,"lamc":0,"lli":0}'], ["Cathode loses", '{"lama":0,"lamc":25,"lli":0}'], ["Lithium is lost", '{"lama":0,"lamc":0,"lli":20}'], ["Quiz case", '{"lama":5,"lamc":3,"lli":2}'], ["All the same", '{"lama":20,"lamc":20,"lli":20}']]) }}
        {{ sl("t4", "lama", "Anode active material lost [%]", 0, 40, 1, 0, 0) }}
        {{ sl("t4", "lamc", "Cathode active material lost [%]", 0, 40, 1, 0, 0) }}
        {{ sl("t4", "lli", "Lithium lost [%]", 0, 40, 1, 0, 0) }}
        {{ sl("t4", "soc", "Charge (Vmin → Vmax)", 0, 1, 0.01, 1) }}
        <button type="button" class="controls__action" data-reset>Reset</button>
      </form>
      <div class="ch-tool__figs">
        <figure><figcaption>The tanks</figcaption><div data-tanks></div></figure>
        <figure><figcaption>Both electrodes against the ceiling</figcaption><div data-electrodes></div></figure>
        <figure class="ch-span"><figcaption>Cell voltage, aged (solid) and fresh (dashed)</figcaption><div data-cell></div></figure>
      </div>
      <dl class="ch-tool__readouts">
        <div><dt>Usable capacity</dt><dd data-readout="capacity">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Capacity lost</dt><dd data-readout="loss">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Scarcest item</dt><dd data-readout="limit">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>N/P</dt><dd data-readout="np">–</dd></div>
        <div><dt>N/Li</dt><dd data-readout="nli">–</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </section>
    <p>If the anode loses active material, its tank gets smaller. If lithium is lost, there's less water. If the cathode loses active material, its tank gets smaller.</p>

    <p>If a cell loses the same share of everything, the ratios stay the same. The OCV looks exactly the same, just with less capacity.</p>

    <details class="math-box">
      <summary>The math behind it</summary>
      <p class="math-legend">{% mi %}\mathrm{LAM}_a, \mathrm{LAM}_c{% endmi %} loss of active material, {% mi %}\mathrm{LLI}{% endmi %} loss of lithium inventory, {% mi %}\Delta x{% endmi %} used window of a tank.</p>
      <p class="math-step">Aged quantities</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      Q_a' &= (1 - \mathrm{LAM}_a)\,Q_a \\[4pt]
      Q_c' &= (1 - \mathrm{LAM}_c)\,Q_c \\[4pt]
      \mathrm{Li}' &= (1 - \mathrm{LLI})\,\mathrm{Li}
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Ratios</p>
      {% math %}
      \begin{aligned}
      \mathrm{N/P}' &= \mathrm{N/P}\,\frac{1 - \mathrm{LAM}_a}{1 - \mathrm{LAM}_c} \\[4pt]
      \mathrm{N/Li}' &= \mathrm{N/Li}\,\frac{1 - \mathrm{LAM}_a}{1 - \mathrm{LLI}}
      \end{aligned}
      {% endmath %}
      <p class="math-step">Capacity</p>
      {% math %}Q_\mathrm{cell} = \min\big(\mathrm{Li}',\ Q_c'\,\Delta x_c,\ Q_a'\,\Delta x_a\big){% endmath %}
      <p class="math-legend">Only in the ideal linear case, since the windows {% mi %}\Delta x{% endmi %} themselves depend on Vmin and Vmax.</p>
      <p class="math-step">Equal losses</p>
      {% math %}\mathrm{LAM}_a = \mathrm{LAM}_c = \mathrm{LLI} \;\Rightarrow\; \mathrm{N/P}' = \mathrm{N/P},\ \ \mathrm{N/Li}' = \mathrm{N/Li}{% endmath %}
    </details>
    <p class="chapter-next"><a href="#n-li-ratio">← N/Li ratio</a></p>
  </section>
